{"kind":"notes","id":"nt/y90nupkv","slide":1,"text":"Formula convention for the candidate architecture (not a claim about existing code): F has N pixel rows and C channels. Q has N pixel rows and M regional columns, with nonnegative same-building assignment weights. Valid building pixels have row sum 1; background rows are zero. Empty columns are removed. D=diag(Q^T 1_N); T=D^{-1}Q^T F, dimension M by C. Local interaction gives deltaT_local, also M by C; T_A=T+deltaT_local. W_local and W_mem are bias-free C by C output projections. deltaF_local=Q(deltaT_local W_local); F_A=F+deltaF_local. Building k_b=Normalize(Proj(Pool_b(T_A))). Each support j provides regional set V_j and building label y_j, source ID_j and mask m_j. U_j=CrossAttn(T_A,V_j,V_j), independently per current building, with parameters shared across supports. Learned support aggregation takes {U_j,e(y_j),m_j} and produces deltaT_mem, dimension M by C. Invalid supports contribute zero; when none are valid, deltaT_mem is explicitly zero (avoid all-masked softmax). deltaF_mem=Q(deltaT_mem W_mem). Yhat=h(F_A+deltaF_mem) denotes four-class pixel scores; argmax yields the discrete map. Q is always pixel-to-region association, not the attention query variable. These equations define a consistent candidate using regional width C; if another width d is used, an explicit input projection is required before the token residual. Native editable math labels use Cambria Math with positioned subscripts. Region/support icons and output example remain schematic. Image provenance and figure inspirations: BRIGHT ESSD 2025 Fig6; Superpixel Transformers https://arxiv.org/html/2309.16889v2 and RAC https://arxiv.org/pdf/2202.11233. No performance claim."}